irst few pages I was  desperate to do it because it grabbed  those those things in me that I'm always  looking for in a project a little bit  about the audition process now and  looping these to your film because I was  also obviously reading up and an all  Arabic casts using the dialect as well  it sounds like oh it could be a big  challenge for you when you speak the  language but it also sounds like you  guys made the right decision  yeah without a doubt I and and initially  I think it struck a lot of people as a  huge challenge  I think it's the only way to do a movie  like this so we went truly all around  the world looking for this talent that  we have in the cast we couldn't get  anyone directly out of Iraq because we  pre-production is right when the travel  ban madness was happening in the United  States so everything was kind of frozen  we couldn't get them out of Iraq and I  think if we could have getting them into  Morocco was a months-long proposition so  we went everywhere Adam is from France  he's Tunisian Sohail was born and raised  in Baghdad but now lives in Albuquerque  New Mexico was a refugee and made it to  the United States we have a kid from  Detroit we have five from Amman Jordan  we had people from Egypt from Iraq and  it was just a question of can we in this  diaspora can we find you know 12 14  really great people for the SWAT team  and and I think we did that and it was  literally Seraphin and Lara Tala and  Amman Jordan it was a global effort and  I think they they did a spectacular job  I cannot wait to see everybody in action  in this movie Sohail I was reading a  little bit about  your career overall and just like the  highs making it in America what was it  like having this script in this  opportunity cross your path at this  point in your career thank you for  everything I think it's really is a good  opportunity for us like in Arab actors  and this is I think it's the first time  that's happening and in Hollywood they  they have a